1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,464
Nearly every expensive upgrade you crave

2
00:00:02,464 --> 00:00:04,189
right now will start to feel

3
00:00:04,189 --> 00:00:06,900
like background wallpaper surprisingly fast.

4
00:00:06,900 --> 00:00:07,404
In 1971,

5
00:00:07,404 --> 00:00:10,491
psychologists Philip Brickman and Donald Campbell

6
00:00:10,491 --> 00:00:12,507
described the hedonic treadmill:

7
00:00:12,507 --> 00:00:14,585
people adapt to nicer apartments,

8
00:00:14,585 --> 00:00:15,278
newer cars,

9
00:00:15,278 --> 00:00:16,475
and bigger screens,

10
00:00:16,475 --> 00:00:18,869
and their happiness drifts back toward

11
00:00:18,869 --> 00:00:19,940
where it started.

12
00:00:19,940 --> 00:00:22,355
The financial trap is asymmetric.

13
00:00:22,355 --> 00:00:24,477
The dopamine rush of a luxury

14
00:00:24,477 --> 00:00:26,892
lease evaporates in three months,

15
00:00:26,892 --> 00:00:29,380
while the fixed monthly bill stays

16
00:00:29,380 --> 00:00:31,210
locked in for five years.

17
00:00:31,210 --> 00:00:33,224
To escape without feeling deprived,

18
00:00:33,224 --> 00:00:34,951
practice Intentional Contrast:

19
00:00:34,951 --> 00:00:36,793
keep your monthly baseline lean,

20
00:00:36,793 --> 00:00:39,556
then enjoy occasional debt-free experiences that

21
00:00:39,556 --> 00:00:41,570
stay special because they are rare.

22
00:00:41,570 --> 00:00:43,918
Run a seven-day simplicity reset every

23
00:00:43,918 --> 00:00:46,265
quarter so ordinary comforts feel rich

24
00:00:46,265 --> 00:00:48,860
again and your savings compound untouched.
